==Records==
===Acceleration and braking record===
[[File:Bugatti Chiron IMG 0087.jpg|thumb|Bugatti Chiron 0-400-0]]
[[File:Bugatti Chiron, IAA 2017, Frankfurt (1Y7A2856).jpg|thumb|The time taken for the run on the side of the car]]

At the 2017 IAA show in Frankfurt, Bugatti announced that the Chiron broke the record of fastest {{Convert|0-400-0|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}} acceleration time, completing it in 41.96 seconds in a span of {{Convert|2|mi|km|1|abbr=off|order=flip}} at the [[Ehra-Lessien]] high-speed oval on a weekend in August 2017.<ref>{{cite press release |title=0-400-0 km/h in 42 Seconds: Bugatti Chiron Sets World Record |url=https://newsroom.bugatti.com/press-releases/0-400-0-km-h-in-42-seconds-bugatti-chiron-sets-world-record |access-date=23 December 2022 |publisher=Bugatti |date=11 September 2017 |location=Molsheim/Frankfurt}}</ref> The car was driven by [[Colombia]]n racing driver [[Juan Pablo Montoya]].<ref>{{Citation | last=Bugatti | title=BUGATTI Chiron 0-400-0 km/h in 42 seconds – A WORLD RECORD #IAA2017 | date=2017-09-12 | url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkkV1vLHUvQ | access-date=2017-09-14 | archive-date=2017-09-13 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170913224424/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkkV1vLHUvQ | url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/frankfurt-motor-show/watch-bugatti-chiron-go-0-249mph-0 | title=Watch the Bugatti Chiron go from 0-249mph-0 | website=www.topgear.com | date=12 September 2017 | language=en | access-date=2017-09-14 | archive-date=2017-09-13 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170913221138/https://www.topgear.com/car-news/frankfurt-motor-show/watch-bugatti-chiron-go-0-249mph-0 | url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news | url=http://www.businessinsider.com/bugatti-chiron-set-new-speed-record-2017-9 | title=The $3 million Bugatti Chiron just set a new speed record | work=Business Insider | access-date=2017-09-14 | language=en | archive-date=2017-09-13 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170913121500/http://www.businessinsider.com/bugatti-chiron-set-new-speed-record-2017-9 | url-status=live }}</ref>

Bugatti also added an extra livery to the Chiron on display to confirm that it was the same car that broke the {{convert|0–400–0|km/h|0|abbr=on}} record.<ref name=":1">{{Citation | last=ExpovistaTV | title=Bugatti Chiron Makes Land Speed Record. | date=2017-09-12 | url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wTAism6n3Ho | access-date=2017-09-14 | archive-date=2020-11-08 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108104910/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wTAism6n3Ho | url-status=live }}</ref> During the show, Bugatti also mentioned that during the run, the car accelerated from {{Convert|0-62|mph|kph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}} in 2.4 seconds, {{Convert|0-124|mph|kph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}} in 6.1 seconds, {{Convert|0-186|mph|kph|abbr=on|order=flip|round=5}} in 13.1 seconds, and {{Convert|0-248.5|mph|kph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}} in 32.6 seconds, which altogether, also makes the Chiron faster than its predecessor, the Veyron.<ref name=":1" />

The record has since been broken by the [[Koenigsegg Regera#World record|Koenigsegg Regera]] and other vehicles.

===Top speed achievement===
On 2 August 2019, Bugatti test driver [[Andy Wallace (racing driver)|Andy Wallace]] achieved a [[Production car speed record#Cars excluded from the list|speed]] of {{cvt|490.484|kph|mph|abbr=on}} in a pre-production [[Bugatti Chiron Super Sport 300+|Chiron Super Sport 300+]] prototype at [[Ehra-Lessien|Volkswagen's test facility in Ehra-Lessien]]. The speed was verified by the [[Technischer Überwachungsverein|TÜV]], Germany's Technical Inspection Association. In its final production form, the Chiron SS 300+ has different seats, no roll-cage, standard ride height and an electronically limited top speed of {{cvt|440|kph|mph|1}}.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2019-09-02 |title=Bugatti has broken the 300mph barrier |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/tg-awards-2019/bugatti-has-broken-300mph-barrier |access-date=2023-04-05 |website=Top Gear |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |first=Andrew |last=Sluys |date=2019-09-03 |title=Watch: Bugatti Chiron hits 490km/h in a record-breaking speed run |url=https://www.driven.co.nz/news/watch-bugatti-chiron-hits-490km-h-in-a-record-breaking-speed-run/ |access-date=2023-04-05 |website=Driven}}</ref>

The car was developed under Bugatti head exterior designer Frank Heyl in collaboration with Italian automobile engineering firm [[Dallara]] and tyre manufacturer [[Michelin]] over the course of six months. The overall length of the car was increased by {{cvt|9.8|in|mm|1|abbr=on|order=flip}} and it is fitted by a laser-controlled ride height system in order to reduce drag. To further reduce drag and aid aerodynamics, the electronically controlled rear wing was removed and was replaced with a long tail incorporating a rear wing with a short cross-section. A full roll cage was added for safety reasons, and the passenger seat was removed to make way for data recording equipment used to validate the record run. The car incorporates an exhaust system first introduced on the [[Bugatti Centodieci]] in order to further reduce aerodynamic drag. Mechanically, the engine was modified to generate {{cvt|1177|kW|hp PS|0|abbr=on}} of power output with no changes to the gearbox and all-wheel-drive system.<ref name="S484"/><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/tg-awards-2019/bugatti-has-broken-300mph-barrier|title=Bugatti has broken the 300mph barrier|date=2019-09-02|website=Top Gear|language=en|access-date=2022-05-15|archive-date=2019-09-02|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190902080725/https://www.topgear.com/car-news/bugatti-has-broken-300mph-barrier|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/supercars/bugatti-chiron-super-sport-300-production-300mph-car|title=The Bugatti Chiron Super Sport 300+ is a production 300mph car|date=2019-09-08|website=Top Gear|language=en|access-date=2022-05-15}}</ref>

